artifact.self_contained_problem:
A finite-dimensional billiard system consists of geodesic motion on a Riemannian manifold with boundary, with elastic reflection (equal angles of incidence and reflection) along the boundary. It is classical that billiard flows arise as limits of geodesic flows on manifolds where a tubular neighborhood of a hypersurface is collapsed, so billiards and geodesic flows are closely related.

In infinite dimensions, many equations of mathematical physics are realized as geodesic equations of right-invariant metrics on infinite-dimensional Lie groups (Euler–Arnold equations). For example:
 (i) the Euler equation of an ideal incompressible fluid is the geodesic equation of the right-invariant L^2 metric on the group of volume-preserving diffeomorphisms of a Riemannian manifold;
 (ii) the KdV equation arises as a geodesic equation on the Virasoro group with a right-invariant L^2 metric;
 (iii) similar geodesic interpretations exist for the equations of magnetohydrodynamics, the Heisenberg magnetic chain, and other PDEs.

Problem. Construct physically meaningful examples of infinite-dimensional billiard systems, that is, geodesic flows with elastic reflection along a codimension-one "wall" (a smooth hypersurface, or a suitable subset on which a reflection law can be defined) in an infinite-dimensional Riemannian manifold or Lie group equipped with a right-invariant metric, such that the resulting reflecting geodesic dynamics corresponds to (or arises naturally from) an equation of mathematical physics — for instance a constrained or reflected variant of the Euler equation of hydrodynamics, KdV, magnetohydrodynamics, or another Euler–Arnold geodesic equation. Specify the underlying group/manifold, the right-invariant metric, the reflecting hypersurface, the reflection law, and the associated PDE.

